[THOUGHT] The supervisor explicitly identified five critical missing data points required to complete the evidence framework and rule out mimics or complications:  
(1) SpO₂ on room air and supplemental O₂ — needed to assess respiratory compromise severity and guide oxygen therapy;  
(2) Arterial blood gas (ABG) with pH, pCO₂, HCO₃⁻, base excess — essential to detect metabolic acidosis (e.g., from shock, sepsis, or lactic acidosis) or respiratory compensation;  
(3) Serum lactate — gold-standard marker of tissue hypoperfusion/shock;  
(4) Focused abdominal exam: rebound tenderness, rigidity, bowel sounds — to assess for peritonitis (e.g., from mediastinal contamination extending to abdomen) or ileus;  
(5) Cardiac auscultation: murmurs, rubs, muffled heart sounds — to exclude cardiac tamponade, pericarditis, or aortic dissection (though less likely, must be ruled out given chest pain and shock).  

All five are *physically exam- or point-of-care test–based* and have not yet been obtained. Since prior ABG/lactate requests returned “not specified” but were not declared unavailable, and SpO₂ was explicitly flagged as missing, I will now request **SpO₂ first**, as it is noninvasive, immediately available, and directly informs respiratory status — a key determinant in Boerhaave management (e.g., need for intubation if hypoxic). Simultaneously, I must obtain the physical exam elements — but per atomic inquiry rule, I can only ask one thing per turn. SpO₂ is fastest and highest yield to triage next steps.

[TEST] Please measure oxygen saturation (SpO₂) on room air and again with 15 L/min via non-rebreather mask.

